<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
        <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Pick It Up!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures to act out daily classroom routines (e.g., pretending to sit down when asked).
Can clarify instructions by asking for repetition when needed (e.g., saying 'Repeat, please' when a command is not heard or understood).
Can use drawings to illustrate daily classroom routines (e.g., drawing a picture of a student reading).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-20px 0 10px 0;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
                <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game focused on classroom routines. "Simon says, 'Sit down!'" "Simon says, 'Pick up a pen!'" "Simon says, 'Clap your hands!'" This activates prior knowledge and reviews vocabulary and imperatives through TPR.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models acting out simple routines and linking them to verbal commands. For example, the teacher holds up a paper and a crayon, then says "Draw on paper," and makes a drawing motion, then shows a picture of a student drawing. The teacher models asking for repetition: "Repeat, please?" with a hand gesture (cupping ear).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher performs an action (e.g., picking up a toy) and asks, "What am I doing? Pick it...?" (Expecting 'up').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a chair and asks, "What do we do here? We...?" (Expecting 'sit').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a drawing of a child jumping and asks, "Is this 'jump' or 'sit'?" (Expecting 'jump').</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards illustrating a sequence of classroom routines (e.g., "Step 1: Sit down on the chair.", "Step 2: Take out paper and a pen.", "Step 3: Draw a picture.", "Step 4: Pick up your pen and put it in the bag."). The visuals clearly depict the actions and objects (chair, desk, pen, paper, bag). The teacher pronounces /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down' clearly, and focuses on recognizing letter sounds A, B, C, D, E by pointing to letters on an alphabet chart related to the drawn items or actions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing daily routines: Use simple language to describe classroom routines with numbers. Following instructions: Respond to routines with actions, such as 'pick up' by picking up materials after a lesson.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a sequence of 3-4 routine cards and asks, "What happens first? What happens next?" Students point to the correct card.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shuffles the cards and asks students to help put them in the correct numerical order (Step 1, Step 2, Step 3).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a card showing a routine and asks, "What verb is this?" (e.g., for "Pick it up," students might say 'pick up' or act it out).</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will mediate understanding between verbal commands and actions/drawings. Grammar: Present simple ('This is a pen.'), Sequencing words with numbers ('Step 1, Step 2'), Imperatives ('Pick it up.'). Vocabulary: sit, play, take, put, pick up, draw, chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, notebook, on, up, down, Please. Pronunciation: /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down'.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Act It Out & Match" (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher says a routine command (e.g., "Please, sit down on your chair."). Students perform the action and point to a corresponding drawing of that routine from a set of cards on their desk. The teacher then asks, "What is Step 1?" encouraging them to mediate the action to the numbered sequence.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Draw & Show" (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays a drawing of a classroom routine (e.g., a child picking up a pen). Students identify the action and say the command (e.g., "Pick up the pen!"). If a student doesn't understand, they are prompted to say, "Repeat, please?" The teacher then repeats clearly, emphasizing /p/ in 'pick' and /d' in 'down'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Routine Sequence Guessing" (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a small set of mixed routine drawings/flashcards. Student A secretly chooses a routine drawing and acts it out using gestures only. Student B watches and tries to guess the routine, saying the command (e.g., "Draw on paper!"). Then they swap. Students practice mediating actions to words and vice-versa.</li>
                    </ol></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Routine Role Play" (21st Century Skills Project). Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group chooses 2-3 daily classroom routines to perform (e.g., "Sit down, open notebook, draw a picture," or "Pick up a bag, put it on a chair, take out a pen."). They will first draw simple illustrations for each step of their chosen routine sequence. Then, they will present their "Routine Role Play" to the class, using their drawings as visual aids and acting out the routine while one student verbally describes the steps using sequencing words and imperatives (e.g., "Step 1: Sit down, please!").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "Pick It Up!" and Scenario: "Our Routines":</b> Students integrate previously learned vocabulary (chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, notebook, draw, sit, take, put, pick up) into their routine sequences. The focus for mediation is for students to effectively link their drawings, gestures, and simple spoken commands to clearly communicate the routine to their classmates. They also practice clarifying instructions by using polite expressions ('Please') and by asking 'Repeat, please?' if a group member is unclear.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in simple utterances, collaborative interaction to plan and execute the role play, and communicative task completion through mediated presentation (drawings + gestures + words).</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Routine Role Play" to the class. Students will show their sequence drawings, act out the routines with gestures, and use simple commands with sequencing words (e.g., "Step 1: Sit down, please! Step 2: Draw a big picture."). Other students can ask clarifying questions using "Repeat, please?" for the performers to mediate by repeating or re-demonstrating.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down' and other key vocabulary.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes at least 3-4 target words (e.g., chair, pen, paper, pick up, sit, draw).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of mediated message:</b> Use of gestures to clearly show actions, and drawings to illustrate routines.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of sequencing words:</b> Correctly uses "Step 1, Step 2..." for their routine.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Polite expressions:</b> Uses 'Please' when giving commands.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification:</b> Attempts to use "Repeat, please?" when necessary (either as a speaker or listener).</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a small paper with two faces (happy/sad) and two boxes (gestures/drawings). They circle the happy face if they could show the routine with gestures, and again if they could draw it. They also identify if they used 'Please'. This gives them a chance to reflect on their mediation skills.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to show with your hands during the routine role play?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What routine do you like to do in class the most? How do you say 'Please' when asking for something?</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Routine Role Play" effectively integrated mediation through gestures and drawings. Students grasped sequencing words with numbers, especially with visual support. The "Repeat, please?" prompt was successfully used by a few students, indicating emerging self-advocacy for clarification. Some groups needed more scaffolding to link their drawings to verbal commands consistently. For next steps, more practice with varied commands and objects would be beneficial.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson consolidates routines. The next unit will introduce new vocabulary related to school supplies and places in the school, building on the ability to follow and describe simple sequences. We can use the learned 'Pick it up' command for new objects.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [Time]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>